Subject: [PATCH next 0057] thunderbolt: stream: host conservation counters

When a host stream stalls or fails, nothing tells whether frames were
lost in DMA, never harvested, never re-posted or never read. Add per
stream counters, read through a new read-only ConfigFS attribute
'counters': sessions, RX posted / completed / canceled / damaged /
delivered / failed re-posts, TX posted / completed / canceled, and the
first terminal RX error of the session with the number of frames
delivered before it.

Each counter has exactly one writer context (the RX callback, the TX
callback, or code holding sdev->lock), so a plain WRITE_ONCE() increment
is enough; the attribute reads them with READ_ONCE() and takes no lock.
No lock or atomic is added to any data path. Documented invariant, at
settled points (after a stop returned, before the next start):
posted == completed + canceled and delivered <= completed. Failed
re-posts are counted per frame. Each field is read without a lock: no
coherent multi-field snapshot.

---
--- a/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
+++ b/drivers/thunderbolt/stream.c
@@ -152,6 +152,7 @@
  * @in_owned: @in_hopid is allocated on the attached XDomain by this device
  * @out_owned: @out_hopid is allocated on the attached XDomain by this device
  * @misc_registered: @misc was registered (item release deregisters only then)
+ * @ctr: Host data-path conservation counters, see struct tbstream_counters
  * @ring_size: Size of the rings
  * @throttling: Interrupt throttling rate in ns
  * @busy_poll: Instead of interrupts, busy poll both rings
@@ -201,6 +202,58 @@
  * @gpu_last_epoch: Last accepted peer-agreed DMA epoch (when enabled)
  * @gpu_context: Open or quarantined DMA context (when enabled)
  */
+/**
+ * struct tbstream_counters - Host data-path conservation counters of a stream
+ * @sessions: Start attempts of the host rings (opens and resumes, failed
+ *	      attempts included); each resets the first_error fields
+ * @rx_posted: RX frames given to the ring: priming and successful re-posts
+ * @rx_completed: RX frames completed by the controller
+ * @rx_canceled: RX frames canceled when the ring stopped
+ * @rx_damaged: Completed RX frames with a CRC error or buffer overrun
+ * @rx_delivered: RX frames fully consumed by read() (CLOSE included)
+ * @rx_repost_failed: Delivered RX frames whose re-post failed (per frame:
+ *		      a failed batch of n counts n; CLOSE included)
+ * @tx_posted: TX frames given to the ring (CLOSE included)
+ * @tx_completed: TX frames completed by the controller
+ * @tx_canceled: TX frames canceled when the ring stopped
+ * @first_error: First terminal RX error the reader met since the last start
+ *		 attempt (-EIO: damaged frame, latched before it is consumed;
+ *		 else the re-post error, after delivery), 0 if none. Damage
+ *		 nobody read and start/TX errors are not recorded here.
+ * @first_error_rx: @rx_delivered (a lifetime total, not per session) when
+ *		    @first_error was recorded
+ *
+ * Totals since the stream device was created, except the first_error fields.
+ * Each counter has exactly one writer context: the RX callback (or its NAPI /
+ * manual-poll equivalents, serialized by rx_lock / sdev->lock), the TX
+ * callback, or code holding sdev->lock. Each field is read with READ_ONCE()
+ * without a lock: no torn value, but no coherent snapshot across fields.
+ * Posted counts are incremented after publication, so a callback can run
+ * first: conservation holds at settled points only. After a stop has
+ * returned and before the next start, rx_posted == rx_completed +
+ * rx_canceled and rx_delivered <= rx_completed; the same holds for TX.
+ */
+struct tbstream_counters {
+	u64 sessions;
+	u64 rx_posted;
+	u64 rx_completed;
+	u64 rx_canceled;
+	u64 rx_damaged;
+	u64 rx_delivered;
+	u64 rx_repost_failed;
+	u64 tx_posted;
+	u64 tx_completed;
+	u64 tx_canceled;
+	s64 first_error;
+	u64 first_error_rx;
+};
+
+/* One writer per counter (see struct tbstream_counters): no atomics needed */
+static inline void tbstream_count(u64 *counter, u64 n)
+{
+	WRITE_ONCE(*counter, *counter + n);
+}
+
 struct tbstream_dev {
 	struct config_group group;
 	struct tbstream *stream;
